-- #1111 A-2.6, the BYSTANDER: "feature + UNRELATED code still behaves".
--
-- A-2.6 adds entries to a program-global overlay list that is registered LAST-WINS
-- over the whole-graph constructor and record-info tables.  The failure mode that
-- shape produces is never "my feature is broken" — it is "my feature works and
-- something that never touched it broke", and no gate in this tree asks that
-- question.  So: the overlay FIRES here (`bysa.{Mark}` names a constructor, and
-- `bysz` puts a second bare `Mark` in the universe), and the BYSTANDING assertions are
-- about `bysc`, a module that imports neither of them and shares no name with either.
-- (⚠️ "every assertion" was the old wording and it is wrong — see the MIXED CELL note
-- below; line 49 asserts the feature, not a bystander.)
--
-- All four files must bucket to ZERO diagnostics.  `bysc`'s `Color`/`shade`/`widen`
-- must resolve, typecheck and stay exhaustive exactly as if `bysa`/`bysz` were not
-- in the graph.
--
-- 🚨 THIS FILE IS A MIXED CELL, NOT A PURE BYSTANDER — corrected after measurement.
-- An earlier revision of this header claimed the counterfactual "comes out GREEN …
-- deleting A-2.6's mechanism leaves this fixture PASSING".  THAT IS FALSE, and it was
-- asserted rather than run.  Measured on a binary cold-bootstrapped from pristine
-- `main` 26585d3c — which is exactly "A-2.6 removed", the branch having already merged
-- everything else in `main` — this file is **RED**, exit 1:
--
--   main_bystander.mdk:<the `unMark (Mark 1)` line>: Type mismatch: Tagged vs Other
--   main_bystander.mdk:<same line>:              Type mismatch: Int literal vs String
--
-- (Line NUMBERS deliberately not quoted: this header's own length sets them, so a
--  comment edit would rot them — the trap AGENTS.md records for fixture line counts.)
--
-- The `unMark (Mark 1)` line below is a DRAIN assertion, not a bystanding one:
-- `bysa.{Mark}` is a non-`(..)` member naming a constructor, which is precisely the
-- rule A-2.6 adds, and without it `Mark` falls through to `bysz`'s `Mark String`.
--
-- So read this file as TWO assertions with different jobs:
--   * the `shade`/`widen`/`zid` lines (`bysc` and the unrelated `bysz` fn) — the genuine
--     BYSTANDER half.  Green on `main` and on every variant; they pin "unrelated code
--     still behaves".
--   * the `unMark (Mark 1)` line — a DRAIN, red on `main`, green with A-2.6.
-- Do not cite this fixture as evidence that removing the feature changes nothing.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT SEE — corrected, because the earlier claim here was
-- measured FALSE and it mattered.  This header used to say it was sensitive to "an
-- overlay that registers MORE decls than the unit binds".  It is not, and it
-- STRUCTURALLY CANNOT BE: the overlay is per-module and built from the module's own
-- `DUse` list, so a module that imports nothing — which is exactly what makes `bysc`
-- a bystander — has no overlay to over-register.  The proof is not hypothetical: the
-- first cut of A-2.6 shipped TWO over-registration defects (the overlay out-ranking
-- the importing module's own `prog0`, and a within-module first-match picking a
-- sibling decl by constructor name) and THIS FIXTURE STAYED GREEN through both —
-- measured on a binary built from f6c2d391, exit 0, both halves passing.
--
-- Over-registration is caught instead by fixtures where the over-registered name
-- collides with something the unit really declares or really imports:
-- `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_local_decl_wins/`,
-- `.../ctor_scope_ctors_prefer_type_name/`, `.../ctor_scope_type_only_sibling_decl/`
-- and `test/engine_fixtures/ctor_scope_local_ctor_beats_stdlib_import.mdk`.  Each of
-- those goes RED on the broken binary; this one does not, and that is the honest
-- division of labour: a bystander pins "unrelated code still behaves", nothing more.
import bysa.{Tagged, Mark, unMark}
import bysz.{zid}
import bysc.{Color(..), shade, widen}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = println (shade Red)
  let _ = println (shade Green)
  let _ = println (widen 41)
  let _ = println (unMark (Mark 1))
  println (zid 2)
